      ⍝ Bugs30 #2 (Blake McBride): A containing a →N with no enclosing
      ⍝ defined function used to hang the interpreter forever (→N was
      ⍝ misresolved as a jump to the ⎕EB macro's own line 1, i.e. the
      ⍝ very statement that runs A, looping). Fixed: no enclosing
      ⍝ function means SYNTAX ERROR (like a bare ⍎'→1'), not a hang.

      '→1' ⎕EB '2+2'
SYNTAX ERROR+
      →1
      ^

      )SIC

      '→0' ⎕EB '2+2'
SYNTAX ERROR+
      →0
      ^

      )SIC

      '→¯1' ⎕EB '2+2'
SYNTAX ERROR+
      →¯1
      ^

      )SIC

      ⍝ →N with a real enclosing function must still branch for real.

      )ERASE FOO

∇Z←FOO
      A←'→3' ⎕EB '2+2'
      Z←100
      Z←999
∇

      FOO
999

      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents

      )ERASE FOO

